WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:23.955
Big numbers are awkward to write. A study might collect thirty nine million, nine hundred and eighty thousand responses. Standard form is a short way to write it. A number in standard form is a number from one up to, but not including, ten, times ten to a power. Here that is three point nine nine eight times ten to the power seven.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:38.475
An estimate means round the numbers first, then calculate. Round each to one or two significant figures. Four point eight times nine point seven becomes five times ten, which is fifty.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:57.772
Decimal places count digits after the decimal point. Significant figures count from the first digit that is not zero. Take nought point nought four five six seven. To two decimal places it is nought point nought five. To two significant figures it is nought point nought four six.

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:09.362
To round, look at the next digit. Five or more rounds up. This question type may ask for a total, a difference, an estimate, standard form, or a rounded answer.

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:28.389
Step one: spot the form the answer must take, such as standard form, an estimate, or two decimal places. Step two: do the calculation with exact numbers, or rounded numbers for an estimate. Step three: round at the end, to the stated precision.

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:36.167
The trap is calculating exactly when the question says estimate. An estimate uses rounded numbers.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:51.760
Here is a real exam question. A patient drinks twelve point eight one nine two four units of alcohol. We give that number to two decimal places. It is worth one mark.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:02:02.530
Step one. Spot the form. Two decimal places means we keep two digits after the point. That keeps twelve point eight one.

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:11.282
Step two. Look at the next digit. It is nine. Nine is five or more, so we round up.

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:22.400
Step three. Round up the last kept digit. One becomes two. So twelve point eight one becomes twelve point eight two.

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:35.758
A tempting wrong route is to cut the number off after two decimal places. That gives twelve point eight one. Cutting ignores the next digit. Rounding does not.

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:44.065
Now the exam-ready answer. Twelve point eight two units. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:45.200 --> 00:03:05.032
A common mistake in standard form is a first number of ten or more. Thirty nine point nine eight times ten to the power six has the right value, but thirty nine is more than ten. Move the point once more to get three point nine nine eight times ten to the power seven.

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:27.616
Now you try. Becca times males on two tasks. Their mean time was fifty seven point two four seconds on one task and sixty three point five one seconds on the other. Find the difference, to two significant figures. Write your answer, then choose Continue to Answer.

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:38.961
First find the difference using the exact numbers. Sixty three point five one minus fifty seven point two four is six point two seven seconds.

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:52.163
Now round to two significant figures. The first two digits are six and two. The next digit is seven, which is five or more, so round up. The answer is six point three seconds.

00:03:52.720 --> 00:04:06.807
A common wrong answer leaves it as six point two seven. That is two decimal places, not two significant figures. The question asked for two significant figures, so the answer must be six point three.

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:14.494
So spot the form asked. Calculate with exact numbers. Round at the end.
